Here’s a non-exhaustive summary of what’s going on and how I would improve it:

1. No user existence check or token persistence  
   • Bug: You call await db.findUser(email) but never check if it returned anything. If the email isn’t in your DB you’ll still generate a token and call sendResetEmail (which may blow up or leak that the user doesn’t exist).  
   • Fix: Always check for existence, and only generate & store a token when a real user is found. Respond with the same generic “OK” message whether or not the user exists (this prevents user enumeration).

2. Insecure, predictable “token”  
   • Bug: `Math.random().toString(36).substr(2)` is neither cryptographically strong nor guaranteed unique.  
   • Fix: use Node’s crypto module to generate an unpredictable, sufficiently long random blob:  
     ```js
     const crypto = require('crypto');
     const rawToken = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex');  // 256 bits
     ```

3. Never store the token or expiration  
   • Bug: You generate a token but you never write it to the database, so you have no way to verify it later.  
   • Fix: in the same transaction where you generate your random token, save a hashed version (so if your DB is dumped an attacker can’t trivially hijack resets) along with a creation or expiration timestamp (e.g. 1 hour out).  
     ```js
     const hashedToken = crypto
       .createHash('sha256')
       .update(rawToken)
       .digest('hex');

     await db.saveResetToken({
       userId: user.id,
       tokenHash: hashedToken,
       expiresAt: Date.now() + 1000 * 60 * 60  // 1h
     });
     ```

4. No rate-limiting or abuse controls  
   • Issue: An attacker can flood this endpoint to spam email addresses or probe your system.  
   • Fix: add a rate limiter (per IP or per email address) using e.g. express-rate-limit.  

5. No input validation/sanitization  
   • Issue: `req.body.email` could be missing / malformed.  
   • Fix: run it through a validator (e.g. express-validator or Joi) and return a 400 if it’s not a valid email string.

6. No error handling  
   • Bug: if db.findUser or sendResetEmail throws, you’ll crash or leak a stack trace.  
   • Fix: wrap in try/catch and return a 500 with a generic message (`{ success:false, error:"Internal error" }`); log the real error on the server only.

7. Generic “success” response to prevent enumeration  
   • Once you’ve done the above, you should *always* return the same status and body (e.g. 200 `{ success: true }`) whether or not the email was in your system.

Putting it all together you end up with something like:

```js
const express = require('express');
const crypto  = require('crypto');
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');
const { body, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

const resetLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 minutes
  max: 5,                   // limit each IP to 5 requests per windowMs
  message: { success: false, error: 'Too many requests, please try later.' }
});

app.post(
  '/api/reset-password',
  resetLimiter,
  body('email').isEmail().normalizeEmail(),
  async (req, res) => {
    // input validation
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
      return res.status(400).json({ success: false, error: 'Invalid email' });
    }

    const { email } = req.body;
    try {
      const user = await db.findUserByEmail(email);

      if (user) {
        // 1) generate a cryptographically secure token
        const rawToken = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex');
        const tokenHash = crypto
          .createHash('sha256')
          .update(rawToken)
          .digest('hex');
        const expiresAt = Date.now() + 1000 * 60 * 60; // 1 hour

        // 2) store hashed token + expiry
        await db.saveResetToken({
          userId: user.id,
          tokenHash,
          expiresAt
        });

        // 3) send email with the *raw* token link
        await sendResetEmail(email, rawToken);
      }

      // ALWAYS return success to avoid leaking which emails exist
      res.json({ success: true });
    } catch (err) {
      console.error('Reset-password error:', err);
      res.status(500).json({ success: false, error: 'Internal server error' });
    }
  }
);
```

Key takeaways:
- Always use crypto.randomBytes for security-sensitive tokens
- Persist (hashed) tokens plus expiry so you can verify them later
- Rate-limit and validate inputs
- Don’t reveal whether an email address exists
- Catch and handle errors rather than letting them bubble up to the client.
